-- R5, field opening (IMPLICIT-PROPS-DESIGN.md §11.7): two record-typed
-- binders of one rule that share a field name make a bare read of that name
-- ambiguous. The error names both binders, at the read and at the declaration
-- that opened the second; `buyer's name` and `seller's name` both still work.
--
-- Merely sharing the name is fine: `who pays` below opens the same two records
-- and reads them spelled out, and draws no error.

DECLARE Buyer  HAS name IS A STRING
                   pays IS A NUMBER
DECLARE Seller HAS name IS A STRING

GIVEN buyer  IS A Buyer
      seller IS A Seller
GIVETH A STRING
`who signs` MEANS name

GIVEN buyer  IS A Buyer
      seller IS A Seller
GIVETH A STRING
`who pays` MEANS buyer's name

-- A field only one of them has is not a collision.
GIVEN buyer  IS A Buyer
      seller IS A Seller
GIVETH A NUMBER
`how much` MEANS pays

-- Two field names shared by the same pair of binders draw ONE error at the
-- later binder, naming both, rather than two stacked on the identical range.
DECLARE Agent HAS name IS A STRING
                  pays IS A NUMBER

GIVEN buyer IS A Buyer
      agent IS A Agent
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
`both` MEANS name EQUALS "x" AND pays > 0

-- Three binders: the message degrades from "Neither one" to "None of them",
-- and the earlier binders are joined with an "and".
DECLARE Vector HAS x IS A NUMBER

GIVEN a IS A Vector
      b IS A Vector
      c IS A Vector
GIVETH A NUMBER
`sum` MEANS x

-- Two NESTED sections are ONE tier of the rank (§11.7 has a single rung for
-- "fields opened from" section GIVENs), so a name both open collides here too
-- rather than the inner one silently winning.
DECLARE Person HAS age IS A NUMBER
DECLARE Pet    HAS age IS A NUMBER

§ `Outer`
    GIVEN owner IS A Person

§§ `Inner`
    GIVEN animal IS A Pet

GIVETH A NUMBER
`whose age` MEANS age
